  1| \chapter{DC Circuits: Kirchhoff's Laws and Theorems}\label{ch:b1:dc-circuits}
  2| 
  3| Turn the key on a cold morning: the starter groans, the dashboard lights
  4| dim for a second, and the car coughs into life. The battery that
  5| delivered a steady \qty{12.6}{V} a moment ago is down to eleven while
  6| the starter draws its hundred and fifty amperes --- and the headlights,
  7| wired in parallel, pay for it. Everything in that second is governed by
  8| two conservation laws and a handful of theorems about linear circuits,
  9| which this chapter states, proves, and applies: Kirchhoff's laws, the
 10| models of sources and resistors, dividers, Thévenin and Norton, and
 11| the graphical art of finding an operating point.
 12| 
 13| \section{Current, voltage, and the quasi-steady regime}
 14| 
 15| \begin{definition}[Electric current]\label{def:b1:dc-circuits:current}
 16| \index{electric current}\index{ampere}
 17| The \emph{electric current} through a surface (a wire's cross-section)
 18| is the charge crossing it per unit time, counted positively in a chosen
 19| orientation:
 20| \[
 21| i = \frac{\dd q}{\dd t} \qquad (\text{amperes: } \qty{1}{A} = \qty{1}{C/s}).
 22| \]
 23| Reversing the orientation changes the sign of $i$. In a metal the
 24| carriers are electrons, which drift \emph{against} the conventional
 25| current direction, at a fraction of a millimeter per second.
 26| \end{definition}
 27| 
 28| \begin{definition}[Potential and voltage]\label{def:b1:dc-circuits:voltage}
 29| \index{electric potential}\index{voltage}\index{ground}
 30| Each point of a circuit has an \emph{electric potential} $V$ (volts),
 31| defined up to a constant fixed by choosing a \emph{ground} ($V = 0$).
 32| The \emph{voltage} between $A$ and $B$ is the potential difference
 33| $u_{AB} = V_A - V_B$, drawn as an arrow from $B$ to $A$. The energy a
 34| charge $q$ loses in going from $A$ to $B$ is $q\,u_{AB}$ (this connects
 35| to the electrostatic potential of \cref{ch:b1:potential-capacitors}).
 36| \end{definition}
 37| 
 38| \begin{definition}[Quasi-steady regime (ARQS)]\label{def:b1:dc-circuits:arqs}
 39| \index{quasi-steady regime}\index{ARQS}
 40| A circuit of size $\ell$ driven at frequency $f$ is in the
 41| \emph{quasi-steady regime} when the propagation time $\ell/c$ of
 42| electrical signals along it is negligible against the period $1/f$:
 43| $\ell \ll c/f$. The current is then the same at every point of an
 44| unbranched wire at each instant, and the laws below apply at each
 45| instant to time-varying currents and voltages (lowercase $i$, $u$)
 46| exactly as to steady ones ($I$, $U$).
 47| \end{definition}
 48| 
 49| \begin{example}[How quasi is quasi]\label{ex:b1:dc-circuits:arqs}
 50| At \qty{50}{Hz}, $c/f = \qty{6000}{km}$: a house is in the quasi-steady
 51| regime. At \qty{1}{MHz}, \qty{300}{m}: a radio still is. At \qty{2}{GHz},
 52| \qty{15}{cm}: a phone's circuit board is not, and its tracks must be
 53| treated as transmission lines --- a topic of the Year 2 volume.
 54| \end{example}
 55| 
 56| \begin{theorem}[Kirchhoff's laws]\label{thm:b1:dc-circuits:kirchhoff}
 57| \index{Kirchhoff's laws}\index{node law}\index{loop law}
 58| In the quasi-steady regime:
 59| \begin{enumerate}
 60|   \item \emph{Node law}: at a node where several wires meet, the sum of
 61|         the currents arriving equals the sum of the currents leaving,
 62|         $\sum_{\text{in}} i_k = \sum_{\text{out}} i_k$.
 63|   \item \emph{Loop law}: around any closed loop, the voltages add up to
 64|         zero when counted with the sign given by a chosen direction of
 65|         travel: $\sum_{\text{loop}} \pm u_k = 0$.
 66| \end{enumerate}
 67| \end{theorem}
 68| 
 69| \begin{proof}
 70| Charge is conserved and, in the quasi-steady regime, does not pile up
 71| at a node: what arrives per second leaves per second. The loop law is
 72| the statement that the potential is a function of position: going
 73| around a loop and back to the starting point, the sum of the potential
 74| drops is $V_A - V_A = 0$.
 75| \end{proof}
 76| 
 77| \begin{notation}[Conventions and power]\label{not:b1:dc-circuits:conventions}
 78| \index{receiver convention}\index{generator convention}\index{electric power}
 79| For a two-terminal component (a \emph{dipole}), the \emph{receiver
 80| convention} draws the voltage arrow and the current arrow in opposite
 81| directions; the \emph{generator convention} draws them in the same
 82| direction. In the receiver convention, the power \emph{received} by the
 83| dipole is
 84| \[
 85| p = u\,i \qquad (\text{watts}),
 86| \]
 87| positive for a resistor that heats, negative for a battery that
 88| delivers. In the generator convention $p = ui$ is the power
 89| \emph{delivered}.
 90| \end{notation}
 91| 
 92| \begin{omfigure}
 93| \begin{tikzpicture}[font=\small]
 94|   \draw (0,0) to[generic, l=dipole, v=$u$, i=$i$] (3,0);
 95|   \node[below] at (1.5,-1.0) {receiver convention: $p_{\text{received}} = ui$};
 96|   \begin{scope}[xshift=6cm]
 97|   \draw (0,0) to[generic, l=dipole, v<=$u$, i=$i$] (3,0);
 98|   \node[below] at (1.5,-1.0) {generator convention: $p_{\text{delivered}} = ui$};
 99|   \end{scope}
100| \end{tikzpicture}
101| 
102| {\small The two sign conventions for a dipole: arrows opposed
103| (receiver) or aligned (generator). The physics is the same; only the
104| sign of $ui$ is read differently.}
105| \end{omfigure}
