107| \section{Dipoles and their models}
108| 
109| \begin{definition}[Characteristic; resistor; Ohm's law]\label{def:b1:dc-circuits:resistor}
110| \index{characteristic (of a dipole)}\index{resistor}\index{Ohm's law}\index{Joule effect}
111| The \emph{characteristic} of a dipole is the curve $i(u)$ (or $u(i)$)
112| it imposes. A \emph{resistor} has a linear characteristic through the
113| origin, \emph{Ohm's law}
114| \[
115| u = R\,i \qquad (\text{receiver convention}),
116| \]
117| $R$ being its \emph{resistance} (ohms, $\qty{1}{\ohm} = \qty{1}{V/A}$) and
118| $G = 1/R$ its \emph{conductance} (siemens). It receives $p = Ri^2 =
119| u^2/R \geq 0$, dissipated as heat (\emph{Joule effect}). A wire of
120| length $\ell$, cross-section $S$ and resistivity $\rho$ has
121| $R = \rho\ell/S$.
122| \end{definition}
